package net.portswigger.mcp.tools

/** Observation and diagnosis guidance only; never introduces native controls or request retries. */
internal const val RATE_LIMIT_OBSERVATION_GUIDANCE = """

Rate-limit evidence and continuation decisions:
An HTTP 429 response alone does not justify automatically pausing/cancelling an audit or declaring the entire Scan/configuration failed. First inspect the applicable resource-pool automatic-throttling settings, the actual Task pool assignment, native state/errors and the existing request/response sequence. Burp's automatic throttling adds a short delay after configured responses such as 429; it does not prove the failed request or the same diagnostic check was retried. A saved Default resource-pool setting and concurrency one do not establish the actual Task assignment or observed backoff. This API cannot verify native Task pool assignment; keep that limitation explicit.
Separate a rejected known-good prerequisite login or benign control from an attacked diagnostic request. A 403 caused by intentionally attacking CSRF/state input can be an expected rejection, not a configuration error; a rejected normal prerequisite needs review of the affected interval and whether the target received the intended authenticated workflow. Use observed inputs, stage order and application responses to distinguish them; status codes or zero native transport errors alone cannot decide this. Zero native transport errors does not mean no HTTP 429 responses occurred. Later 2xx/3xx responses at the same destination show later responses only: even HTTP 200 can be a login failure page. They do not prove authentication/application recovery or that earlier missed checks were repeated. Preserve affected and unverified checks in the report instead of relabeling them as passed or assuming the entire audit is invalid.
Observe existing traffic and wait for the observed restriction to clear as appropriate to the user's authorization and target behavior. Do not invent automatic retries, repeat uncertain mutations, weaken protections, reset counters or silently exclude attacked fields. Keep native timing-control failure handling and existing approval/Scan controls unchanged. This guidance sends no requests and grants no extra continuation, retry or control permission.
"""
